\subsection{Diagnostic Framework}
\label{sec:diagnostic-framework}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

\looseness=-1 Three zero-cost diagnostics predict the outcome of any self-extraction-based aggregation method from a $K = 12$ pilot run: (1) the degenerate-regime indicator $C_0 < 5\%$ (SICA reduces to SC); (2) the diversity floor $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.30$ (insufficient vote diversity for any aggregation gain); (3) the bias ratio BR combined with SC regime (Type~A: SC $\geq 60\%$ with BR $> 1$ predicts confirmation-bias degradation; Type~C: SC $\leq 40\%$ predicts error amplification). These boundaries correctly characterize the failure mode across all 60 tested conditions (16 main configurations, 27 remediation variants on Mistral-7B${\times}$FOLIO, and 17 analytical conditions) within the self-extraction paradigm. The framework's practical value lies in failure-mode diagnosis---degenerate, diversity-limited, or confirmation-biased---from a $K{=}12$ pilot run, enabling practitioners to rule out self-extraction before full deployment. Conversely, when diagnostics detect sufficient capability gap (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}), the framework correctly predicts positive gain ($+6.67$\,pp for Mistral-7B on ProofWriter).

\looseness=-1 Decision boundaries ($C_0 < 5\%$, $K_\text{eff} \leq 1.30$, SC $< 40\%$) were derived from Mistral-7B and Qwen2.5-14B. We evaluate prospectively on two held-out generators: LLaMA-3.1-8B (different family) on FOLIO, where the framework predicts Type~A bias and the observed $\Delta = -4.41$\,pp confirms; and Qwen3-14B (same family, different generation) on FOLIO and ProofWriter, likewise confirmed ($\Delta \leq +1.47$\,pp, n.s.).

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

\looseness=-1
Our central finding is that the value of formal verification depends on the \emph{independence} of its inputs, not the soundness of its logic. \emph{SICA-specific}: under source-attribution scoring, SICA reduces to majority vote (Observation~1), and 27 training-free variants fail to escape. \emph{General}: the DPI bound applies to any deterministic self-extraction, and symbolic confirmation bias---a persistent empirical pattern across all tested zero-shot self-extraction conditions, not a cognitive bias analogy---holds across nine models (7B to frontier), suggesting the bottleneck is intrinsic to self-referential constraint generation. Three diagnostics ($K_\text{eff}$, BR, $C_0$) correctly characterize failure modes across all tested conditions, with boundaries generalizing to held-out families (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic-framework}).

\looseness=-1
These results connect to ensemble diversity theory \citep{dietterich2000ensemble, krogh1994neural}: with $K_\text{eff} \leq 3.62$ for $K{=}12$, same-model traces scale redundancy, not information. For test-time compute scaling \citep{snell2025scaling}, increasing $K$ within a single model yields diminishing returns when traces share systematic biases.

\looseness=-1
Escaping the bottleneck requires structural independence (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}); cross-architecture NLI provides a proof of concept, but whether trained extractors or hybrid approaches can shift the boundary remains open. Our diagnostic framework offers a concrete tool: a $K{=}12$ pilot run with three zero-cost metrics predicts whether any self-extraction method will help before full deployment.
